d other labs  that maybe  haven't been as engaged to do that  because  it moves from just being an and  you know a couple of labs doing this to  more and more labs doing this  to becoming something that the  department's doing and that culture  moves along  and so i don't know um you know  with within a particular lab though i  mean if you're a member of that lab  that's hard um but setting that example  maybe just as  one person in the lab and sharing what  you're doing to be efficient and then  seeing if there's another person  that was also willing to be do their  part to be efficient  and seeing if you can grow that culture  internally  uh within the lab over time  you know as i mentioned culture shifts  can take time so  um you know if there's there's efforts  like that that you can do  i have seen on my campus  just labs that really in the beginning  would avoid  um you know me when i walk down the hall  but that with the the cultural shift  within the building and  and that over time those labs  have turned into some of our most  engaged labs  so this culture shift is slow it takes  time as a building process and um  building through examples i think is a  good way  to try to move that and i hope that  answered your question  christine i don't know if you have  anything to add i mean you were so  um  yeah i mean one one thing that comes to  mind is you know if you're  if you're in a lab that is hoarding  reagents samples  in in cold storage units um  i think maybe approaching that issue in  your laboratory from the perspective  of i'm unable to be effective  as a scientist because i say  you know there's a lot of stuff in the  freezer that i don't know what it is or  the refrigerator  whatever it might be there's a lot of  stuff that i don't know what it is and i  have to dig  through it to get my own stuff we're  leaving the door open  if you frame it from like a resiliency  standpoint that might  um get through to your your pi a little  bit better  um another idea is you know to talk with  them about  cost so um you know  if your lab is hoarding stuff and things  are really  full you know you could share that like  instead of buying another unit which  will cost  ten thousand twelve thousand fifteen  thousand dollars and be a significant  expense for the lab  if you all just took some time to kind  of sort through what you have  might be able to free up space and  eliminate that  that you know costs that will be coming  down the line  if your lab doesn't take action with  their with their cold storage  so those are just a couple ideas too if  you need to frame it differently  to appeal to what's important to your pi  um obviously you know trying to be  energy efficient  is great and um you know doing your best  on that front but that doesn't always  speak to everybody so trying to frame it  in a different way might help  thank you both so much for this  excellent q a session we are out of time  and we had a significant amount of  questions that we are when  we are unable to answer during this live  event but i just do want to remind our  audience that those questions we didn't  answer  and those that come in during the  on-demand period they will be addressed  by our speakers  and our team at brooks life sciences via  the email address you provided at the  time of registration  i once i would like to once again thank  dr kathy ramirez aguilar and christina  griever for their time today and their  important contribution to reducing  environmental footprint of freezer use  and research  um do either of you have any final  comments for our audience  yeah i really appreciate it go ahead oh  sorry christina go ahead  oh that's okay okay  i know um so christina here um with my  green lab  and i just want to you know mention if  you do have any you know follow-up  questions that you didn't get in you  know you can  submit them and we'll answer them during  the on-demand period for the webinar  um please visit the my green lab website  if you're curious more about what we do  we have many different programs the  freezer challenge is just one of them  like the lab certification i mentioned  we do some work on green chemistry  the act label which is all about  sustainable procurement so we have a lot  to offer  um you can sign up for our monthly  newsletter and  on our website and then we're also on  twitter facebook and linkedin  so and i manage that social media so if  you interact with us  you're interacting with me for the most  part and um that'd be really great  moving forward  and thanks again everybody for for  listening in on this  again i just wanted to thank everyone as  well for listening in  um you know working on efficient cold  storage has been so rewarding at cu  boulder and  and i and i hope that you'll find the  same on your campus  uh and if you're interested in more you  can visit the  the cu green labs um page uh and we have  lots of resources on freezers  and and many other efforts as well and  and i also encourage you  to attend the i2sl conference in chicago  in in  october so it's really a  fun and and grassroots in many way  experience and and so just there's so  much passion  around sustainability and labs i i just  love going every year  and and hearing about all the great  efforts that people are doing and one of  the  nicest things about i2sl conference is  that you're getting such a diverse  range of stakeholders attending i mean  it includes engineers designers  architects  but also safety folks and and  individuals like christina and i working  on with scientists and also scientists  sometimes  come as well so i encourage you to  attend and share what you're doing  thank you both and thank you to  labyrinths and our sponsor brooks life  sciences for underwriting today's  educational webcast  if you are interested in receiving your  continuing education credits  please click on the continuing education  credits tab located at the top right of  your presenta